{"type":"image","url":"/textbook/build/m04-iqr-criterion-1c1fb5e4df8547918631b7c976a49d6a.svg","alt":"A number line with Q1, the median M, and Q3 marked in the middle. Red boundary lines mark Q1 minus 1.5 IQR on the left and Q3 plus 1.5 IQR on the right. The shaded zones beyond these boundaries are labeled suspected outliers.","key":"mIR2o4As2g","urlSource":"images/gen/m04-iqr-criterion.svg"}],"enumerator":"1","key":"HmmEOZGhYP"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example: Best Actress Oscar Winners","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"abHC3fEqDO"}],"key":"ch6KYZQww6"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We will continue with the Best Actress Oscar winners example:","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"QM59F4vpsM"}],"key":"NIPQDsvaVR"},{"type":"code","lang":"","value":"34 34 27 37 42 41 36 32 41 33 31 74 33 49 38 61 21 41 26 80 42 29\n33 36 45 49 39 34 26 25 33 35 35 28 30 29 61 32 33 45 29 62 22 44","position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"key":"lvJdImyc6x"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Recall that when we first looked at the histogram of ages of Best Actress Oscar winners, there were 5 observations that looked like possible outliers:","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"R5YKBpil7U"}],"key":"swZPHOJ4ZD"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/textbook/build/m04-oscar-outliers-a43a0757c274efb348a50906cc8c5a1c.svg","alt":"The histogram of winners' ages with the bars at ages 60 to 64 (three winners), 70 to 74 (one winner), and 80 to 84 (one winner) shown in red and labeled possible outliers.","key":"LHTYxVmFm2","urlSource":"images/gen/m04-oscar-outliers.svg"}],"enumerator":"2","key":"ExD7RHHrnm"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We can now use the 1.5(IQR) criterion to check whether the 5 observations should indeed be classified as outliers:","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"key":"VLb4GUmVsF"}],"key":"NV3mJM6YfJ"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For this example we found that Q1 = 30.5 and Q3 = 42, so IQR = 11.5","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"key":"g4pFu04XqC"}],"key":"mYIOUHUZS1"}],"key":"kARHdVZNSZ"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineMath","value":"Q1 - 1.5(IQR) = 30.5 - (1.5)(11.5) = 13.25","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>30.5</mn><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>11.5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>13.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q1 - 1.5(IQR) = 30.5 - (1.5)(11.5) = 13.25</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8778em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">Q</span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1.5</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.07847em;\">I</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.00773em;\">QR</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">30.5</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">1.5</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">11.5</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">13.25</span></span></span></span>","key":"MdcFepRMBf"}],"key":"I48MR1jyFs"}],"key":"PhMQdoL2RC"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineMath","value":"Q3 + 1.5(IQR) = 42 + (1.5)(11.5) = 59.25","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>42</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>11.5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>59.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q3 + 1.5(IQR) = 42 + (1.5)(11.5) = 59.25</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8778em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">Q</span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1.5</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.07847em;\">I</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.00773em;\">QR</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">42</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">1.5</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">11.5</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">59.25</span></span></span></span>","key":"Gv9999gQyM"}],"key":"ZAgGG7PCi3"}],"key":"moJ5XC7A5A"}],"key":"pJlsD6y33D"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The 1.5(IQR) criterion tells us that any observation that is below 13.25 or above 59.25 is considered a suspected outlier.","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"key":"nuVoWcGtQC"}],"key":"dxj5gm8v7I"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We therefore conclude that the observations 61, 61, 62, 74 and 80 should be flagged as suspected outliers in the distribution of ages. Note that since the smallest observation is 21, there are no suspected low outliers in this distribution.","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"key":"R9k2oajC9E"}],"key":"zzh3wbKR0a"}],"class":"tip","key":"aNPbIhhcbN"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":47,"column":1},"end":{"line":47,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Check Your Understanding: Identifying Outliers with the IQR","position":{"start":{"line":47,"column":1},"end":{"line":47,"column":1}},"key":"WUFW7vCyKH"}],"identifier":"check-your-understanding-identifying-outliers-with-the-iqr","label":"Check Your Understanding: Identifying Outliers with the IQR","html_id":"check-your-understanding-identifying-outliers-with-the-iqr","implicit":true,"key":"gJDypquzyC"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-faeec8a11af0994557177489ef8f052f.mjs","model":{"question":"A dataset has Q1 = 50 and Q3 = 70. According to the 1.5(IQR) criterion, which of the following observations would be flagged as a suspected high outlier?","choices":[{"correct":false,"text":"95","feedback":"95 is below the cutoff of Q3 + 1.5(IQR) = 70 + 30 = 100."},{"correct":true,"text":"105","feedback":"Correct! IQR = 20, so the high cutoff is 70 + 1.5(20) = 100, and 105 exceeds it."},{"correct":false,"text":"85","feedback":"85 is well within the cutoff of 100."}],"multi":false,"hint":"First find IQR = Q3 − Q1, then compute Q3 + 1.5(IQR).","explanation":""},"key":"BvfAbaih1y"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-faeec8a11af0994557177489ef8f052f.mjs","model":{"question":"Using the same dataset (Q1 = 50, Q3 = 70), below what value would an observation be flagged as a suspected low outlier?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"20","feedback":"Correct! Q1 − 1.5(IQR) = 50 − 30 = 20, so observations below 20 are suspected low outliers."},{"correct":false,"text":"50","feedback":"50 is Q1 itself—the cutoff extends 1.5 IQRs below it."},{"correct":false,"text":"35","feedback":"35 would be the cutoff if you used 0.75(IQR); the criterion uses 1.5(IQR)."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Compute Q1 − 1.5(IQR).","explanation":""},"key":"t7LdF7uz6N"}],"key":"EbcQm2jFPk"}],"key":"u2dvffu2U3"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"The Interquartile Range in Action","url":"/pages/m04-measures-of-spread-3-of-5","group":"Section 3: Examining Distributions"},"next":{"title":"Outliers: When to Investigate, Keep, or Discard","url":"/pages/m04-measures-of-spread-5-of-5","group":"Section 3: Examining Distributions"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}